[[:db/retract 17592186162661 :question/answer-fields 17592186161258] [:db/retract 17592186162682 :question/answer-fields 17592186161384] [:db/retract 17592186162688 :question/answer-fields 17592186161420] {:db/id 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5d3ee109-94d4-53ed-8971-ac2ef97dce27" "question-70b4f245-e7d8-5908-9e68-f829b0901af8" "question-d974fc39-79e3-5801-98d4-4a560ca244ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161808 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e56fbd2ced76bc02d7c9b29a81f3afab000053d11ec878040f26cef6cc5f65ce.svg)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/ba/ba1b9ecff7b4825d7eb4fa25ad706c14d04a82b8daa45e524d556c90d71700ca.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161810 :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb8af25c129aac1ef9268d108c073e4b895f5e154c26ddb5692fd527d4eaddc3.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/8c/8c5b88c719ee0e5495e9b0df2c9f4add97a668e74249680fbf378c7c775d92f2.svg)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/b3/b36812322e6f9128de625a8a2d4e7faf62ba98fb007224c819e2a05738cd7f98.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/16/1608541af7edcbbb06504c97b907dc2c40a97d9a7c9575d5145c67785f9eb56c.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9020d5f8-c5f3-54dc-9474-2d2b14ec7c2a" "question-2b4092bd-68de-5340-8ca8-436b2e1233eb" "question-b33c0902-677a-58a2-8ff6-887b643a37e9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161809 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0cb7d70ea0921d1e2e8f2f0a9d8bc06427f29e28b8ed6ea79b5db4ba132f1e0.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2a2f1d5d-f0eb-595a-8c0e-cc29b31a1a50" "question-345be953-89e4-5540-a9e1-d54a38da924f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161811 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb6e660e7dd1c933fd2d5c6b6a05fbdf1fe7f5f8ed24faf15f88ea16d7d04889.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/74/748df21fe8314e02f5a5253edaa39427c38600cd60099ccdfed075a28499d23e.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/08/08573d10464e356dc2eeb7a3a0a4c5f9255d0f96b14da9d63b718b766c1cc8d8.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/252867b0bccd95be918f05320ee65c7153f499b15ccc77624be18128d0f0de09.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d3ee109-94d4-53ed-8971-ac2ef97dce27" :question/id #uuid "5d3ee109-94d4-53ed-8971-ac2ef97dce27" :question/math-academy-id "q-95901" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Σ$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $U$ is a diagonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $3\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix,the matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $U$ must be orthogonal but not necessarily diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-deb04f7a-e9c5-545a-b755-ad9c8bf9de09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-deb04f7a-e9c5-545a-b755-ad9c8bf9de09" :answer-field/id #uuid "deb04f7a-e9c5-545a-b755-ad9c8bf9de09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99ad27ff-65f7-5f3d-abde-578637efa421" :answer-field/choices ["answer-edad4670-ba38-528d-afdc-042641739a6d" "answer-7718b520-dda1-5f3a-bc7b-0d6b3cb9898b" "answer-766d0c5a-5abc-5b7d-9ced-05213b2666d0" "answer-99ad27ff-65f7-5f3d-abde-578637efa421" "answer-ad41e705-b0fd-57cc-9d10-850ea2d27a07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edad4670-ba38-528d-afdc-042641739a6d" :answer/id #uuid "edad4670-ba38-528d-afdc-042641739a6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7718b520-dda1-5f3a-bc7b-0d6b3cb9898b" :answer/id #uuid "7718b520-dda1-5f3a-bc7b-0d6b3cb9898b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-766d0c5a-5abc-5b7d-9ced-05213b2666d0" :answer/id #uuid "766d0c5a-5abc-5b7d-9ced-05213b2666d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99ad27ff-65f7-5f3d-abde-578637efa421" :answer/id #uuid "99ad27ff-65f7-5f3d-abde-578637efa421" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad41e705-b0fd-57cc-9d10-850ea2d27a07" :answer/id #uuid "ad41e705-b0fd-57cc-9d10-850ea2d27a07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-70b4f245-e7d8-5908-9e68-f829b0901af8" :question/id #uuid "70b4f245-e7d8-5908-9e68-f829b0901af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-96233" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69fb243f-fdae-537e-9f2b-a71cb67829a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69fb243f-fdae-537e-9f2b-a71cb67829a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "69fb243f-fdae-537e-9f2b-a71cb67829a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55eda9fc-807f-5348-8813-d854a5b33209" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2a9967c-3445-5f6d-842d-d5d0897a84a0" "answer-55eda9fc-807f-5348-8813-d854a5b33209" "answer-781d2783-b360-5c7a-a6be-21fa965bb5ee" "answer-dc3ebc96-2def-5db3-af96-e1b883783089" "answer-6fb3ccbb-6f9a-5137-a3b4-d0a87f0e7267"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2a9967c-3445-5f6d-842d-d5d0897a84a0" :answer/id #uuid "e2a9967c-3445-5f6d-842d-d5d0897a84a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55eda9fc-807f-5348-8813-d854a5b33209" :answer/id #uuid "55eda9fc-807f-5348-8813-d854a5b33209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-781d2783-b360-5c7a-a6be-21fa965bb5ee" :answer/id #uuid "781d2783-b360-5c7a-a6be-21fa965bb5ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc3ebc96-2def-5db3-af96-e1b883783089" :answer/id #uuid "dc3ebc96-2def-5db3-af96-e1b883783089" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fb3ccbb-6f9a-5137-a3b4-d0a87f0e7267" :answer/id #uuid "6fb3ccbb-6f9a-5137-a3b4-d0a87f0e7267" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d974fc39-79e3-5801-98d4-4a560ca244ac" :question/id #uuid "d974fc39-79e3-5801-98d4-4a560ca244ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-95902" :question/problem "$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-088b86a3-f34a-5ba8-80a9-ee349d3833cb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-088b86a3-f34a-5ba8-80a9-ee349d3833cb" :answer-field/id #uuid "088b86a3-f34a-5ba8-80a9-ee349d3833cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18398a20-b6f3-582f-801a-647783315331" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aede1490-5d8e-5534-9bfa-eda0cd6308d8" "answer-90e858fa-4eea-5813-82b7-faa7293717a6" "answer-7d56d147-9186-53b6-94cd-9e814942e315" "answer-18398a20-b6f3-582f-801a-647783315331" "answer-862b4360-497c-53df-909d-2ca8fe8f54dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aede1490-5d8e-5534-9bfa-eda0cd6308d8" :answer/id #uuid "aede1490-5d8e-5534-9bfa-eda0cd6308d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90e858fa-4eea-5813-82b7-faa7293717a6" :answer/id #uuid "90e858fa-4eea-5813-82b7-faa7293717a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d56d147-9186-53b6-94cd-9e814942e315" :answer/id #uuid "7d56d147-9186-53b6-94cd-9e814942e315" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18398a20-b6f3-582f-801a-647783315331" :answer/id #uuid "18398a20-b6f3-582f-801a-647783315331" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-862b4360-497c-53df-909d-2ca8fe8f54dd" :answer/id #uuid "862b4360-497c-53df-909d-2ca8fe8f54dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162682 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{32}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c09611e5-05b8-5cb4-967b-28cb8b87db79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c09611e5-05b8-5cb4-967b-28cb8b87db79" :answer-field/id #uuid "c09611e5-05b8-5cb4-967b-28cb8b87db79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eccfbb1d-63d6-5847-8131-30535d50b607" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82cad0d2-c8f3-56cd-8aac-7b3ceabb2dc7" "answer-eccfbb1d-63d6-5847-8131-30535d50b607" "answer-987dbc1f-e284-5329-a013-0a5967179f36" "answer-3ba44f9d-452d-5c1b-a60d-1fa5aefc80dd" "answer-780c2e8d-9e3d-5f3e-8162-9411863f1b43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82cad0d2-c8f3-56cd-8aac-7b3ceabb2dc7" :answer/id #uuid "82cad0d2-c8f3-56cd-8aac-7b3ceabb2dc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eccfbb1d-63d6-5847-8131-30535d50b607" :answer/id #uuid "eccfbb1d-63d6-5847-8131-30535d50b607" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-987dbc1f-e284-5329-a013-0a5967179f36" :answer/id #uuid "987dbc1f-e284-5329-a013-0a5967179f36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ba44f9d-452d-5c1b-a60d-1fa5aefc80dd" :answer/id #uuid "3ba44f9d-452d-5c1b-a60d-1fa5aefc80dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-780c2e8d-9e3d-5f3e-8162-9411863f1b43" :answer/id #uuid "780c2e8d-9e3d-5f3e-8162-9411863f1b43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9020d5f8-c5f3-54dc-9474-2d2b14ec7c2a" :question/id #uuid "9020d5f8-c5f3-54dc-9474-2d2b14ec7c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96305" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8cefc262-fc57-5df7-b114-66f69510f234"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8cefc262-fc57-5df7-b114-66f69510f234" :answer-